\section{Introduction}

Neural networks have become a widely adopted model for a wide range of prediction tasks, yet their internal representations remain largely opaque.
A central challenge in interpretable machine learning is understanding not only
which features influence a model's output, but how features combine to do so.
Feature interactions : cases where the joint effect of two or more features on the output cannot be decomposed into a sum of their individual contributions. These feature interactions are a primary source of this complexity and are of direct scientific interest in domains where understanding the structure of relationships is as important as
predictive accuracy \citep{tsang2020featureinteractioninterpretabilitycase,
enouen2023sparseinteractionadditivenetworks}. In practice, correctly identifying
which feature combinations drive a prediction is critical in high-stakes settings
such as credit scoring, medical diagnosis, and algorithmic hiring, where
regulators and practitioners increasingly require explanations that go beyond
individual feature importance \citep{NEURIPS2020_443dec30}.


Detecting these interactions reliably is therefore a prerequisite for meaningful
interpretability. Neural Interaction Detection (NID), proposed by
\cite{tsang2017detecting}, addresses this by reading interaction structure
directly from the trained weight matrices of a multilayer perceptron (MLP).
Instead of searching through all possible feature subsets, NID uses the observable statistical interactions routed in shared hidden units.   NID then uses the strength of the weight connecting features to output as a proxy to measure interaction strength.
Because NID operates on the learned weights rather than on model predictions, its
effectiveness is directly tied to the structure that training instils in those
weights.

This creates a dependency that has received limited attention. The
weight structure of a trained network is shaped by every design decision made
during training: the optimiser, the regularisation scheme, the level of noise
present in the training targets, and the network architecture. If dropout
suppresses certain weight paths to prevent overfitting, it may simultaneously
suppress the paths that encode interactions. If label noise prevents the model
from accurately fitting the target signal, interaction structure may never be
strongly encoded in the weights in the first place. While
\cite{lengerich2021dropoutregularizerinteractioneffects} establish that
dropout and \(L_2\) regularisation shape interaction learning in distinct ways,
and \cite{arpit2017closerlookmemorizationdeep} show that label noise
fundamentally alters the training trajectory, no work has directly measured how
these two families of intervention jointly affect the fidelity of interaction
recovery against a known ground truth, or how recovery evolves across training
epochs rather than at a single convergence point.


This study addresses that gap. Using ten synthetic benchmark functions with
known ground truth interaction sets, adopted from \cite{tsang2017detecting}, a
factorial experiment is conducted across five output noise levels ($\eta \in
\{0, 0.1, 0.2, 0.5, 1.0\}$), two optimisers, two regularisation
configurations (dropout and weight decay), and two MLP architectures. NID is
applied not only at the best-validation-loss checkpoint but at every saved
epoch, producing a full recovery trajectory for each condition. Three
dimensions of interaction recovery are examined: the \textit{recovery level},
defined as the AUPRC achieved at the best epoch; the \textit{recovery
trajectory}, defined as the sequence of AUPRC values across training epochs;
and \textit{stability}, defined as the consistency of recovery across
the ten benchmark functions under the same experimental condition, measured by
the standard deviation of per-function AUPRC.


The main findings are as follows. Dropout substantially reduces interaction
recovery, lowering mean AUPRC by \statDropoutDrop\ relative to an
unregularised baseline, while weight decay is considerably more benign
($-$7.1\%). Output noise lowers the attainable recovery plateau without
fundamentally changing the early trajectory shape, suggesting that interaction
structure is encoded during the early phases of training before label noise
fitting begins to compete for representational capacity. Validation loss is a
weak proxy for interaction recovery quality ($\rho = \statSpearmanRho$,
$p < 0.001$), implying that standard loss-based model selection does not
reliably identify models with strong interaction structure. Smaller architectures
match or outperform the deeper model on the majority of benchmark functions,
with the largest gaps appearing under adverse conditions.

The code used to generate the data, train the models, and evaluate interaction recovery is publicly available.\footnote{\url{https://github.com/EdouarddDw/MLP-Interaction-Detection}}



\section{Literature Review}
\label{sec:lit_review}

\subsection{Feature interactions and why they matter}

In the context of neural networks, statistical interactions are formally defined with respect to a set of features \(I\). An interaction exists if the model function \(f(x)\) cannot be decomposed into a sum of arbitrary subfunctions that each exclude at least one variable from \(I\)
\citep{enouen2023sparseinteractionadditivenetworks, tsang2020featureinteractioninterpretabilitycase}. Mathematically, for a feature set \(I = \{x_1, \dots, x_k\}\), a non-additive interaction is present if:
\begin{equation}
f(x) \neq \sum_{i \in I} f_i(x_{I \setminus \{i\}})
\end{equation}

This means the entire feature set must be known simultaneously to correctly predict the output, the features do not act independently \citep{enouen2023sparseinteractionadditivenetworks}. A simple example is a multiplicative term \((x_1 \cdot x_2)\), which cannot be represented by adding separate functions of \(x_1\) and \(x_2\) alone \citep{tsang2020featureinteractioninterpretabilitycase}. It is important to distinguish these non-additive interactions from \textit{pure interactions}, which measure the variance explained by a group of variables that cannot be explained by any strict subset of those variables \citep{lengerich2021dropoutregularizerinteractioneffects}.

Detecting interactions reliably matters beyond academic interest. In domains such as personalised recommendation and ad targeting, interactions between features often drive the most important predictions, and understanding them is necessary for auditing model behaviour \citep{tsang2020featureinteractioninterpretabilitycase}. More broadly, interpretability requirements in applied settings increasingly demand not just which features are important, but how they combine; a model that produces plausible feature importance scores may still misattribute predictions if the underlying interaction structure is incorrectly recovered. Synthetic datasets with known ground truth interactions provide the controlled environment needed to evaluate detection methods rigorously, since researchers can verify whether recovered interactions match the true ones before the method is deployed on real data \citep{agarwal2024openxaitransparentevaluationmodel, wong2021reallytrustyousimple}.

\subsection{Methods for detecting interactions in neural networks}

Several families of methods exist for detecting feature interactions in neural networks, and they differ substantially in what they measure and how they measure it. Prediction-based methods such as Friedman's H-statistic \citep{friedman2008predictive} quantify interactions by measuring how much the joint partial dependence of two features deviates from the sum of their individual effects. These methods are model-agnostic but computationally intensive and sensitive to the choice of reference distribution \citep{friedman2008predictive}.

Attribution-based methods, including Shapley interaction values and integrated gradient decompositions \citep{lundberg2017unified}, distribute a model's output back to input pairs and provide theoretically grounded interaction estimates, but typically require many forward passes and can be unstable for higher-order interactions \citep{enouen2023sparseinteractionadditivenetworks}.

The third family of interaction detection builds interpretability methods directly into the model architecture. Sparse interaction additive networks \citep{enouen2023sparseinteractionadditivenetworks} and neural additive models \citep{agarwal2021neural} constrain the model structure to make interactions explicit by design. Since this interpretability is by model design, it cannot be used post hoc on a pre-trained model.

The fourth family is proposed by \cite{tsang2017detecting} and uses weights to read interaction structure. Neural Interaction Detection (NID) detects interactions directly from the learned parameters of a trained feedforward network. The core insight is that statistical interactions must be routed through shared hidden units; any interaction present in the network's function requires at least one hidden unit whose ancestor set at the input layer contains all members of that interaction \citep{tsang2017detecting}. NID scores candidate interactions by aggregating the product of upstream and downstream weight magnitudes, using the minimum weight in an interaction path as a bottleneck measure. Compared to prediction-based and attribution-based alternatives, NID is fast and requires no additional forward passes, making it practical for trajectory evaluation across many epochs.

Its dependence on the learned weight structure also makes it directly sensitive to any training intervention that changes how weights are distributed. Importantly, \(L_1\) regularization is required to produce the sparse, interpretable weight paths that make this detection feasible \citep{tsang2020featureinteractioninterpretabilitycase}, without sparsity, many paths carry non-negligible weight and the interaction signal is diluted.

Because interaction detection is an imbalanced retrieval problem, the number of true interactions is small relative to the full candidate space of all possible feature subsets. Ranking-based evaluation metrics are more informative than accuracy-based ones. With \(p = 10\) features, the space of possible interactions of size two or more contains 1,013 candidates, but the synthetic functions used in \cite{tsang2017detecting} have only three to five ground truth interactions.

Under this imbalance, a detector that assigns high scores to a few true positives before any false positives should be rewarded, which is precisely what the Area Under the Precision-Recall Curve (AUPRC) captures. AUPRC focuses on the positive class and is insensitive to the large number of true negatives that dominate the candidate space, making it the appropriate primary metric for highly imbalanced retrieval tasks of this kind \citep{davis2006relationship}.

\subsection{Training dynamics: regularisation and interaction learning}

The weight structure that NID reads is not fixed by the architecture alone, it is shaped throughout training by the regularisation scheme applied. The theoretical contrast between dropout and weight decay is important for understanding why the two techniques affect interaction recovery differently.

Dropout randomly removes hidden units during each forward pass, preventing any single pathway from becoming overly specialised \citep{lengerich2021dropoutregularizerinteractioneffects}. From the perspective of interaction learning, this is consequential: statistical interactions require stable co-activation of multiple input features through a shared hidden unit \citep{tsang2017detecting}. When dropout stochastically disables units, it disrupts the formation of consistent multi-feature pathways, effectively penalising the network for relying on any particular interaction route.

\cite{lengerich2021dropoutregularizerinteractioneffects} found that dropout acts as an order-specific regularizer that decays the ability of the model to learn higher-order interactions and forces the model to learn simpler additive patterns over more complex ones.

This forces the model to prefer simple additive patterns over complex interactions. The interaction recovery finds fewer higher-order interactions even if there are genuine higher-order interactions in the data. Weight decay applies an \(L_2\) penalty that shrinks all weights towards zero proportionally \citep{Goodfellow-et-al-2016}. Weight decay thus reduces all weights without removing pathways or disturbing ranking or interaction strengths.

When too large, \(L_2\) can cause instability by collapsing the model toward constant outputs \citep{lengerich2021dropoutregularizerinteractioneffects}, but at moderate strength it is substantially less destructive to interaction structure than dropout. This contrast between dropout changing which pathways are active and weight decay only changing their magnitude should entail they have different effects on NID interaction recovery.

\subsection{Output noise, memorisation, and recovery trajectories}

There are two types of noise discussed in the literature. Firstly, \textit{input noise} refers to perturbations added to the feature vector \(x\). This type of noise is used as a form of regularization and is mathematically equivalent to using an \(L_2\) penalty on weights \citep{Goodfellow-et-al-2016}.

\textit{Output noise}, also called label noise, refers instead to perturbations added to the target variable \(y\). This study uses the latter: Gaussian noise proportional to the standard deviation of the clean signal is added to the output of each synthetic function. By definition, output noise does not alter the feature distribution; it degrades only the target signal the model is trying to fit. The Goodfellow equivalence does not apply here, and the two regimes cannot be assumed to produce the same training dynamics or the same effects on learned weight structure.

The dynamics of learning under label noise have been studied in the context of memorization. \cite{arpit2017closerlookmemorizationdeep} show that neural networks learn noisy and clean examples at different times. The model, in early phases of training, learns patterns that generalize across many batches before fitting idiosyncratic or noisy patterns.

The fact that the model learns noise after patterns that generalize over many examples implies that this information is encoded into the weights earlier during training than noise-driven perturbations.

The trajectory of interaction recovery over epochs is as important as final checkpoint evaluation. Evaluating a model at its best validation loss only indicates how well it recovered interaction \citep{apicella2026dontstopnowrethinking}, but misses the full trajectory indicating if the recovery was gradual, stable, or if early learning was later degraded \citep{toneva2019empiricalstudyexampleforgetting}. This motivates using AUPRC as a sequence throughout training epochs rather than a single point, which is a methodological addition to previous research.

\subsection{Research gap}

Existing work has studied feature interaction detection, regularisation, and label noise largely as separate problems. Three specific gaps remain.

First, \cite{lengerich2021dropoutregularizerinteractioneffects} establishes that dropout and \(L_2\) regularization affect interaction learning in different ways, but no previous study has measured how these methods' effects on weight structure affect NID's ability to recover interactions and how much impact they have on the ability of the model to recover ground truth interactions.

Secondly, previous research generally evaluates interaction recovery at convergence or the best validation loss. Interaction recovery trajectory through the training epochs provides insights into how the model learns that endpoint analysis lacks, such as early plateaus, late degradation, or condition-specific timing. Interaction recovery through the training process has not been studied before.

Thirdly, interaction recovery and fidelity of models with combinations of regularization and input noise have not been explored. Studies address either the generalization effect of regularization or memorization dynamics under noise, but not their influence on interpretability.

This study aims to address these three research gaps by using NID to measure interaction recovery through 100 training epochs, under combinations of regularization techniques, output noise, architecture, and optimizers, evaluated against synthetic data with known ground truth interactions.


