\subsection{Convergence Restriction}

Not all experimental runs produced meaningful learning. Under high output noise or strong
regularisation, a subset of models failed to reduce their validation loss over the course
of training, indicating that the network never extracted usable structure from the data.
Including such runs in the analysis would mix up genuine interaction recovery with the
behavior of models that simply did not learn, inflating variance and obscuring the effect of the experimental conditions.

To address this, a convergence criterion was applied uniformly across all analyses. For
each run, the validation loss at the first recorded epoch was compared to the validation
loss at the final epoch (epoch 100). A run was deemed non-convergent if the relative decrease did not meet a minimum threshold:

\begin{equation}
    \frac{\ell_1 - \ell_{100}}{\ell_1} < 0.03
    \label{eq:convergence_criterion}
\end{equation}

\noindent where $\ell_1$ and $\ell_{100}$ denote the validation loss at epoch 1 and
epoch 100 respectively. Any run satisfying this condition
was excluded. The threshold of 3\% was chosen to be conservative: it removes runs that are clearly flat while retaining runs that converge slowly or noisily, such as those under dropout regularization.

Figure~\ref{fig:loss_vs_auprc_divergence} illustrates the motivation for this criterion.
At noise~$= 0$, validation loss decreases sharply and AUPRC rises steadily across
training, confirming that the model learns meaningful feature interactions. At
noise~$= 1$, validation loss remains near its initial value throughout all 100 epochs and AUPRC shows no systematic improvement, indicating a complete failure to extract
signal from the data. The contrast between these two conditions makes clear that retaining non-convergent runs would introduce noise without adding information.

