\subsection{Effect of Regularisation Strength on Interaction Recovery}
\label{sec:dose_response}

The preceding analysis fixed dropout at $p = 0.2$ and weight decay at
$\lambda = 10^{-4}$. This leaves open whether the 22.9\% and 7.1\% reductions
reported in Table~\ref{tab:auprc_by_regularization} are particular to those
two settings or representative of a broader proportional relationship. To
probe this, a targeted supplementary sweep was conducted on a representative
subset of three benchmark functions ($f_1$, $f_7$, $f_8$, spanning the low-,
mid-, and high-recovery range), holding all other factors at their cleanest
configuration (small architecture, Adam, $\eta = 0$). Dropout was swept over
$p \in \{0, 0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5\}$ and weight decay over
$\lambda \in \{0, 10^{-5}, 10^{-4}, 10^{-3}, 10^{-2}\}$, with each condition
expressed as the percentage change in best-epoch AUPRC relative to that
function's own zero-strength anchor before averaging across functions.

Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response} shows the two sweeps. The clearest result is
asymmetric. Strong dropout ($p = 0.5$) reduces recovery by roughly 40\%
relative to no dropout, extending the $p = 0.2$ finding to higher rates, but
the decline across intermediate values is not to be ignored and the per-function
spread is large. Weight decay produces no reliable degradation at any tested
strength: mean recovery stays within roughly $\pm 20\%$ of the anchor across
four orders of magnitude of $\lambda$, reinforcing the main result that $L_2$
penalization is benign for interaction recovery. The cross-function standard
deviation widens substantially at intermediate dropout rates and at the
strongest weight-decay setting, indicating that individual functions respond
heterogeneously to regularization strength rather than following a common
curve. At three functions and a single seed, these sweeps characterize the
general empirical shape of the relationship : clear suppression under strong dropout and tolerance of weight decay. Full per-condition values are reported in Appendix~\ref{app:dose_response}.

