\subsection{Joint Effect of Noise and Regularization}

Figure~\ref{fig:summary_heatmap} presents the joint effect of noise level and
regularisation condition on mean AUPRC, averaged across all functions and both
architectures. The heatmap reveals that the two factors interact some what additively:
each independently degrades recovery, and the worst cell
combines both in their most damaging configurations.

The highest mean AUPRC is achieved by the base condition at zero noise (0.59),
and the lowest by weight decay at noise $\eta = 1$ (0.32).
Noise and regularisation impose comparable worst-case penalties:
the largest noise-induced drop (weight decay, $0.58 \rightarrow 0.32$,
a fall of 0.26 from $\eta = 0$ to $\eta = 1$)
is essentially tied with the largest regularisation gap at
a fixed noise level (base versus dropout + weight decay at $\eta = 0$, $0.59 \rightarrow 0.34$,
a gap of 0.25). The effect of noise is, however, highly condition-dependent:
it costs 0.20 points under the base condition and 0.26 under weight decay,
but leaves the dropout conditions almost unchanged
(a drop of only 0.07 for dropout, and a marginal \emph{increase} of 0.02 for dropout + weight decay).
Weight decay is thus the most noise-sensitive regularisation condition,
whereas dropout is the most noise-robust.


Unlike dropout, weight decay tracks the base condition closely at low to moderate noise,
matching it at $\eta \in \{ 0, 0.1, 0.2\}$ and slightly exceeding it at $\eta = 0.5$ 
(0.47 vs 0.45). At $\eta = 1$ this advantage disappears and weight decay falls to 0.32,
below base, consistent with the absence of any learnable signal at the highest noise level.
The broader contrast remains: $L_2$ penalisation stays competitive with the unregularised 
baseline across most of the noise range, whereas dropout depresses recovery throughout,
possibly because weight decay constrains weight magnitude without disrupting the relative
ordering of interaction path strengths that NID reads.




