\subsection{Recovery Trajectories Across Conditions}
\label{sec:trajectory}
Figure~\ref{fig:trajectory_highlight} contrasts the four most informative
trajectory conditions: base versus dropout plus weight decay, crossed with
noise~$= 0$ and noise~$= 1$. Together these four panels illustrate how
regularisation and noise shape recovery dynamics rather than simply rescaling
the final AUPRC.

At noise~$= 0$ under the base condition (top left), recovery is progressive and
sustained. The mean trajectory rises steadily across all 100 epochs, with
individual function trajectories spreading over a wide range but consistently
improving. This confirms that clean data provides sufficient signal for the
network to encode interaction structure throughout training. At noise~$= 0$
under dropout plus weight decay (top right), the trajectory is markedly
different in character: the mean is lower and flat from early epochs, with
several individual functions plateauing near 0.50 for the entire duration of
training. Regularisation in this condition appears to prevent the progressive
refinement seen in the base case, consistent with
\cite{lengerich2021dropoutregularizerinteractioneffects}'s observation that
dropout decays the effective learning rate of higher-order interactions.

At noise~$= 1$ (bottom row), both conditions converge to similarly low and flat
trajectories, though the dynamics differ. The base condition (bottom left)
stabilises near 0.35 from the earliest epochs, showing no meaningful improvement
across training. The dropout plus weight decay condition (bottom right) shows a
more pronounced early rise before plateauing at a comparable level near 0.38.
This suggests that regularisation can temporarily facilitate early interaction
recovery even under heavy noise, perhaps by preventing premature overfitting to
noise patterns, but that this advantage does not translate into sustained
improvement. The full trajectory grid across all noise levels and regularisation
conditions is provided in Appendix~\ref{app:full_trajectories}.

