\subsection{Timing of Peak Recovery}

Table~\ref{tab:peak_epoch} reports the mean epoch at which peak AUPRC is reached
for each combination of noise level and regularisation condition. Two patterns
emerge clearly from the data.

First, the base condition reaches its peak latest at low noise ($40.0 \pm 17.4$
at $\eta = 0$), while dropout plus weight decay peaks earliest ($8.0 \pm 10.7$
at $\eta = 0$). This ordering is consistent with the trajectory shapes in
Figure~\ref{fig:trajectory_highlight}: the base condition continues improving
gradually throughout training, whereas heavy regularisation forces an early
plateau. Dropout alone shows an intermediate pattern, peaking around epoch 16
at $\eta = 0$ and stabilising near epochs 34--42 for moderate noise levels.


Second, peak timing appears to interact with the regularisation condition,
though the large cross-function standard deviations in Table~\ref{tab:peak_epoch} mean these patterns should be read as suggestive rather than firmly established.
For the base and weight decay conditions, peak timing is relatively stable from $\eta = 0.1$
to $\eta = 0.5$, clustering in the range of epochs 38 to 46. At $\eta = 1$,
both peak earlier (22.4 and 25.9), consistent with the absence of a learnable signal preventing any late-epoch improvement.
The dropout conditions do not show this early-peak shift at $\eta = 1$, if anything dropout peaks later.

The large standard deviations throughout the table reflect genuine variability
in peak timing across functions rather than measurement noise in the AUPRC
signal. Functions with complex interaction structures or higher sensitivity to
experimental conditions peak at very different epochs from simpler functions.

