The regularisation findings replicate. Dropout significantly reduces recovery
relative to base (median $\Delta = \statDropoutDropSeeded$,
$r = \statDropoutRBCSeeded$, $p_\mathrm{adj} = \statDropoutPvalAdjSeeded$),
confirming the single-seed result that dropout suppresses the weight structure
NID reads. Weight decay, by contrast, is statistically and practically
negligible: the median difference from base is \statWDDropSeeded{} with a
95\% CI of $[\statWDCILoSeeded, \statWDCIHiSeeded]$ and no significant effect
($p = \statWDPvalSeeded$), supporting the interpretation that $L_2$ penalisation
constrains weight magnitude without dismantling the relative path-strength
ordering. The combined dropout and weight-decay condition shows the largest
reduction of all (median $\Delta = \statDropoutWDDropSeeded$,
$r = \statDropoutWDRBCSeeded$, $p_\mathrm{adj} = \statDropoutWDPvalAdjSeeded$).
This refines the single-seed picture: rather than dropout and dropout+weight
decay being near-equivalent, the replication indicates the two regularisers
compound, with weight decay adding further degradation on top of dropout.

The noise findings also replicate. A Friedman test across the three noise
levels in the base condition is significant
