\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

The objective of this paper was to find how regularization techniques and label noise affect the trajectory and stability of interaction recovery in multi-layer perceptrons, evaluated against ground truth. The results have allowed several conclusions to be drawn, but also reveal a number of limitations that limit the scope of the findings.

\subsection*{Regularisation does not uniformly improve interaction recovery}

A consistent finding throughout all experiments is that regularization does not improve interaction recovery in comparison to an unregularized baseline. Dropout lowers mean AUPRC compared to the baseline by \statDropoutDrop\, and dropout in combination with weight decay has a very similar reduction of \statDropoutWDDrop.

This is consistent with \cite{lengerich2021dropoutregularizerinteractioneffects}, who observe that dropout causes the model to break higher-order interactions, thus lowering the ability of the model to learn these interactions. In the context of interaction detection, this means that the very mechanism intended to prevent overfitting actively suppresses the weight structure that NID relies on to identify interactions.

Weight decay presents a different picture. Its reduction relative to base is modest
($-7.1\%$ averaged across noise levels), and the heatmap in
Figure~\ref{fig:summary_heatmap} shows that weight decay tracks base closely at low to
moderate noise, even slightly outperforming it at $\eta = 0.5$ ($0.47$ vs $0.45$). Only at
the highest noise level does it fall below base ($0.32$ vs $0.39$), consistent with the
 lower learnable signal there. This pattern suggests that $L_2$ penalisation
constrains weight magnitude without dismantling the relative strength differences between
interacting and non-interacting paths, which are what NID ultimately reads.

In the single-seed results, dropout alone and dropout combined with weight decay produce very close mean AUPRC (\statDropoutMeanAUPRC\ and \statDropoutWDMeanAUPRC{}), which initially suggested that dropout is the dominant factor and that weight decay adds little once dropout is applied. The multi-seed replication (Section~\ref{sec:multiseed}) refines this picture: under paired testing across seeds, the combined condition produces the largest reduction of all (median $\Delta = \statDropoutWDDropSeeded$, $r = \statDropoutWDRBCSeeded$), exceeding the effect of dropout alone (median $\Delta = \statDropoutDropSeeded$). The single-seed near-equivalence therefore appears to understate the cost of combining the two regularisers; rather than weight decay being inert once dropout is present, the two effects compound. Dropout remains the principal disruptor in both analyses, but weight decay adds further degradation on top of it.

\subsection*{Output noise lowers the recovery ceiling without changing early dynamics}

Output noise degrades interaction recovery monotonically, but the trajectories in Figure~\ref{fig:auprc_by_noise} suggest that this degradation is better characterised as a ceiling effect than as a disruption of the recovery process itself. All noise levels share the same rapid early rise in the first 10--20 epochs; the trajectories then diverge to different plateaus that they maintain stably for the remainder of training.

These results are in agreement with \cite{arpit2017closerlookmemorizationdeep}, who found that models trained on noisy label data tend to learn real patterns at first before learning noise. If this order is the same, interaction structures are encoded in the weights in the initial phase of training before noise starts to be encoded in later training phases.

Recovery appears relatively robust to low label noise but degrades more readily once noise
exceeds a moderate level: the pooled decline is gradual through $\eta = 0.2$ and then
steepens markedly, with the interval between $\eta = 0.2$ and $\eta = 0.5$ accounting for the
largest single increase in degradation. Caution should be exercised before reading this as a sharp
threshold or regime change, given the substantial between-function variance, but the pattern
is at least consistent with noise variance progressively overpowering the interaction signal
that NID relies on. The two architectures reinforce a familiar capacity--robustness trade-off:
the larger model achieves the strongest recovery in the noise-free setting yet falls below
the smaller model under any noise, and the gap widens as noise grows. This suggests that
added capacity aids interaction recovery only when the signal is clean, and becomes a
liability as label noise increases the possibility to overfit on the noisy signal.
\subsection*{Validation loss is a weak proxy for interaction recovery}

One of the more relevant findings is that validation loss is a poor predictor of interaction recovery quality. The Spearman correlation between the two quantities, computed across all \statRetainedRuns\ convergent runs, is $\rho = \statSpearmanRho$,  \(p < 0.001\). The fact that the correlation is negative confirms that lower validation loss is generally associated with a higher AUPRC.
However, the magnitude of this correlation is weak, and the scatter plot in Figure~\ref{fig:auprc_vs_val_loss} shows that the models achieve AUPRCs spanning almost the full observable range.
This was further comfirmed in section \ref{sec:multiseed}

This finding entails for anyone using NID, or any similar weight-based method, that a model chosen for its validation loss will not reliably be the model with the highest interaction recovery. Evaluation against ground truth, where available, is therefore a necessary complement to standard loss-based model selection.

\subsection*{Smaller models recover interactions more reliably under adverse conditions}

The architecture comparison reveals no systematic advantage for the deeper, wider big model. The small model achieves higher mean AUPRC on seven of ten benchmark functions, and the largest margins are precisely on the functions where recovery is otherwise high ($f_5$, $f_8$, $f_{10}$). This is consistent with the noise analysis: the big model's greater capacity appears to increase its susceptibility to label noise and regularisation, preventing it from reliably reaching the recovery levels that the small model attains with the right conditions.

More complicated functions with lower average AUPRC on both big and small architectures have very close interaction recovery. This entails that model capacity has little effect on the ability of the model to capture interactions on these more complex functions. We can assume that, in these experimental conditions, interaction detection signals are weak enough to cause larger models to capture spurious patterns and noise instead of finding ground truth interactions.

\subsection*{Limitations}

Several limitations qualify the conclusions above. First, the main grid of results is based on single-seed runs, so the variability across functions reported throughout Section~\ref{sec:results} merges genuine structural differences between functions with run-specific randomness. To address this directly, a multi-seed replication of the core grid was conducted (Section~\ref{sec:multiseed}), confirming the dropout, weight-decay, and noise findings under paired significance testing across \statSeededNSeeds{} seeds. This replication was, however, restricted to the small architecture with Adam and the reduced noise set $\eta \in \{0, 0.5, 1.0\}$; the optimizer and architecture comparisons were not re-run across seeds, so claims about those two factors remain single-seed. A full multi-seed replication of the entire factorial design would be needed to extend the stability guarantees to every result.

Second, the exclusive use of benchmark synthetic functions specifically designed to test interactions may give results that are not reproducible using real-world data. Results should not be assumed to generalise directly to applied settings.

Third, NID is treated as the only interaction detection method. The sensitivity of the findings to this choice is not assessed. Alternative detection methods, such as those based on Shapley interaction indices or neural additive models, may respond differently to the same regularization and noise conditions.

Fourth, the convergence filter described in Section~\ref{sec:trajectory_eval} excludes runs where validation loss did not decrease by at least 3\% across training. Even though this threshold is conservative, it may create a selection bias for models with more stable training. This might cause the results to underestimate the failure rate of heavily regularized or high-noise experiments.

Fifth, when classifying detected interactions ($\mathcal{D}$), supersets of ground truth interactions ($\mathcal{G}$) are classified as false positives. This is the strictest interpretation of exact interaction recovery. It penalizes cases when NID recovers the correct feature set embedded within a larger interaction. This may lead to underestimating partial interaction recovery in higher-order interaction functions.

Finally, the noise manipulation in this study adds Gaussian noise to the output variable $y$, not to the input features $x$. This is label or output noise, and its relationship to regularisation is not the same as for input noise. In particular, the formal equivalence between input Gaussian noise and $L_2$ regularisation established by \cite{Goodfellow-et-al-2016} does not apply here. The effects of output noise on interaction recovery trajectories documented in this study should therefore be understood as distinct from the dynamics that would arise under input perturbation.

Several directions for future research follow directly from these limitations. Replicating the experiment with multiple random seeds would allow genuine function-level stability to be separated from run-to-run randomness, and would allow statistical significance testing across experiments. Evaluating these experiments on real-world datasets with partially known interaction structures would further test the generality of the findings.

Comparing NID to other interaction detection instruments, such as Shapley or H-statistics, under the same noise and regularization conditions would establish whether the observed results are specific to weight-based methods or are a concrete limitation of recovery under label noise. Finally, exploring input noise as a distinct experimental factor, by perturbing features rather than labels, would allow a direct empirical comparison between the two noise regimes rather than relying on the theoretical equivalence that motivates combining them.

\section{Conclusion}

This paper studies how regularization techniques and dropout affect trajectory and stability of interaction recover in MLPs, using NID against ground truth.

The main finding is that regularisation does not improve interaction recovery. Averaged
across all noise levels, functions, and architectures, dropout reduces mean AUPRC from the
unregularised baseline of by $22.9\%$, whereas weight decay is
much more benign, falling only to $7.1\%$. The combination of the both weight decay and dropout
changes recovery negligibly ($0.404$to $0.400$).
This indicates that dropout is the dominant disruptor, and that
its effect is largely insensitive to whether weight decay is also present. These results
suggest that standard regularisation practice transfers poorly to the interaction detection
setting: the mechanisms that prevent overfitting in prediction tasks can simultaneously
prevent the network from encoding the weight structure that NID relies on.

Output noise lowers the attainable recovery plateau without fundamentally altering the early
trajectory shape, consistent with the view that interaction structure is encoded during the
first 10--20 epochs of training before noise fitting begins to compete for representational
capacity. Pooled recovery declines gradually through $\eta = 0.2$ and then more steeply,
with the interval between $\eta = 0.2$ and $\eta = 0.5$ marking the largest single drop;
recovery continues to fall at comparable rate to $\eta = 1$, so the pattern is better
described as an accelerating decline than as a sharp threshold, particularly given the
substantial between-function variance. The two architectures degrade similarly, though the
larger model, despite a marginal advantage in the noise-free setting falls below the
small model at every positive noise level and is lowest at $\eta = 1$, indicating that its
extra capacity aids recovery only when the signal is clean.

Taken together, these findings suggest that practitioners seeking to use
weight-based interaction detection should exercise caution when applying
standard regularisation schemes, prefer smaller over larger architectures when
interpretability is the primary goal, and evaluate interaction recovery directly
against available ground truth rather than relying on validation loss as a
proxy. Future work replicated across multiple seeds, extended to real-world
datasets, and comparing alternative detection instruments would strengthen
these conclusions and clarify their scope.


